from mk import mk
mk("VrCSssDOxFc","Wavefield Tutorial Video",
 "wavetable-frames-as-animated-spectral-eq-curve-depth-floor-skew",
 "Wavetable frames as an animated spectral EQ: each frame is a gain curve across the spectrum, with depth, floor, skew, smoothing, low-end bypass and per-channel time offset",
 "Build a spectral 'auto-filter' that drives the whole frequency response from a wavetable: map each frame's shape onto the spectrum as a gain curve (peak/valley up to about ±12 dB at full depth), deepen only the cuts with a floor control, warp the frequency mapping with skew (negative = into lows, positive = highs), smooth sharp frames to avoid spectral ringing, bypass everything below a safe-bass frequency (up to 400 Hz), cycle frames forward/ping-pong/reverse/random at a free or tempo-synced rate (or step keyframes with a gate), and widen by feeding L and R the curve at different times. This is an effect on existing pads, keys, loops and strings. It differs from our spectral-warp vocoder and WT-scratch pad skills, which are oscillators/vocoders, not frame-driven EQ curves.",
 ["Spectral processor: STFT with per-bin gain (Web Audio: AudioWorklet FFT), or an approximation with a bank of peaking EQ bands whose gains follow the frame (implementation note)","A wavetable / set of single-cycle frames (original or drawn; ~128 frames)","Optional tempo clock and DAW automation"],
 [("source","audio","Pad, keys, strings, loop, bass or vocal to be spectrally shaped",True),("wavetable","wavetable","Frames whose shapes become gain curves (original/drawn, not vendor tables)",True),("rate","string","Free speed or synced division (32nd … 12 bars)",False)],
 [("animated_spectral_texture","audio","Source with evolving, wavetable-driven spectral motion"),("exported_wavetable","wavetable","Optional edited table saved as a standard WAV for any synth")],
 ["Spectral processing can ring/artifact on steep frame shapes, especially in the low end. Raise smoothing and/or safe-bass before blaming the source.","Do not modulate speed while transport-locked; position will jump. Use free-running mode for smooth speed modulation.","Upstream plugin latency makes a tempo-synced spectral cycle drift late. Compensate with a chain-offset equal to the upstream latency in ms.","Phase-warp (per-bin phase rotation) and spectral-IR (frame as an impulse response) modes are described only qualitatively in the transcript. Treat their settings as by-ear."],
 [("Insert the processor on the source (or in parallel). Use a wavetable whose frames vary a lot. Each frame is read as a gain curve: left = low frequencies, right = highs.","map","A display shows the current frame shape as an EQ curve that evolves over time"),
  ("Set depth: 0 = unchanged; at maximum a frame peak/valley applies about ±12 dB to that frequency region. Lower depth for subtler curves.","depth","Audible spectral motion that scales with depth"),
  ("Set floor: lowering it stretches the negative (cut) part of the curve towards silence while boosts stay controlled by depth. Use low floor for deep notches or high-pass/low-pass/notch-style frames.","floor","Cuts become deep, dramatic notches without louder boosts"),
  ("Set skew to fit the source: negative spreads the shaping down into bass/low-mids (e.g. bass); positive concentrates it in the highs (hats, cymbals, airy pads).","skew","Same shape, stretched toward lows or highs"),
  ("Raise smooth if sharp frames cause ringing/harsh artifacts (e.g. on vocals). Engage safe-bass (bypass below a set frequency, up to 400 Hz) on bass, full mixes or anything whose fundamental must stay solid.","smooth/safe-bass","Cleaner result; sub and bass stay steady while mids/highs move"),
  ("Choose the motion: trim start/end frames to the range with the character you want (re-adjust speed, since the trim becomes the whole cycle). Set speed (slow = evolving) or tempo-sync (e.g. 8 bars for slow cinematic evolution, quarter notes or faster for flutter). Pick playback forward, ping-pong, reverse, or random (jumps between frames at fixed intervals, beat-aligned when synced).","motion","Slow cinematic sweeps or rhythmic flutter, depending on rate"),
  ("For automation-driven motion, pin to a single frame and draw position automation to land spectral moves on specific moments in the arrangement.","pin","Precisely timed spectral changes"),
  ("Draw your own table: up to 20 keyframes, interpolated to fill all frames (edited tables need faster speeds/smaller divisions for the same feel). Draw a simple high-pass shape and generate the remaining keyframes with a sweep fill (progressive shift without wrap-around), floor down, synced over 4 bars, for a classic analog-style HP sweep. Use a loop crossfade at the cycle boundary; export the table as WAV for other synths.","editor","A smooth HP sweep built from frames; seamless loop point"),
  ("Rhythmic variant: step through keyframes one per beat division instead of interpolating, and add gate (silence a % of each step's tail) for chopped, per-step spectral flavours on leads/arps. Using a keyframe count that doesn't divide the phrase (e.g. 20 vs 4/8 bars) gives polyrhythmic filter shaping.","kf-steps","Each step gets its own spectral shape; gated punctuation"),
  ("Width and layering: add stereo so L and R read the curve at different times (mono compatible). On parallel copies or several tracks with the same table, offset the start phase of each so they move out of sync with the same character. Use dry/wet for parallel blend, output gain to level-match, and a 0 dB limiter for aggressive boosts.","stereo/phase","Wider, non-synchronised movement; level-matched output")],
 ["Depth 0 = null difference vs bypass; full depth gives clearly audible ±12 dB-scale curves.","With safe-bass on, content below the set frequency is identical to the dry signal.","Negative vs positive skew visibly moves the shaping between lows and highs on a spectrum analyser.","A synced cycle stays on the grid after the chain-offset compensates upstream latency."],
 ["spectral","fft","wavetable","eq","auto-filter","stereo-width","tempo-sync","web-audio"],
 note="Developer walkthrough of a commercial spectral plugin; distilled to a portable frame-to-gain-curve recipe. Trial/download CTA dropped.")

mk("ysMPHY5oiGc","Wavefinder Tutorial // Wavetable on Tonverk with OS 1.3.0",
 "dual-wt-interval-locked-per-osc-position-level-animators",
 "Dual wavetable voice with an interval-locked tune/detune pair and a per-oscillator 'animator' on position and level: Reese bass, phasing-in harmony, S&H atmos pad and level-ramp pluck recipes",
 "Patch a two-wavetable-oscillator voice in which tune transposes both oscillators while detune holds osc 2's interval (fifth, fourth, compound fifth, or a few cents). Each oscillator has its own modulator on WT position and on level (bipolar depth from the current position; one-shot envelope shapes or looping, tempo-synced LFO/S&H/random). Use it for four portable recipes: (1) a Reese bass with ~15 cents detune and opposite-direction position modulation; (2) a chord lead where osc 2 at −5 st fades in on a slow ramp-up level shape and brightens as it enters; (3) an atmospheric pad with tempo-synced sample-and-hold on both positions at different speeds, osc 2 at +19 st, and modulated S&H speed for generative bursts; (4) a pluck made from a looping ramp-down level shape. Delta vs our wavetable-crossfade and opposite-vector pads: interval locking plus per-osc level animators as the envelope.",
 ["Two wavetable oscillators (Web Audio: PeriodicWave crossfades or an AudioWorklet WT osc, implementation note)","Per-oscillator modulators with envelope-like one-shot and tempo-synced looping shapes incl. S&H/random","Low-pass filter (as an effect), reverb send, overdrive"],
 [("wavetables","wavetable","Original/free tables: identical saws for Reese; saw + metallic FM; airy/hollow + noisy; noisy brightening layer",True),("notes","midi","Bass line, chords, or plucked pattern",True)],
 [("dual_wt_patch","audio","Reese bass, harmonised lead, atmospheric pad or WT pluck")],
 ["Tune moves both oscillators; detune offsets osc 2 only, so the interval survives transposition.","Position modulation depth is bipolar and starts from the current base position. Negative depth subtracts.","Too much position movement thins the low end of basses. Keep it subtle and slow there.","Values beyond those named (±15/±10 cents, −5/+7/+19 st) are by ear."],
 [("Load a wavetable on each oscillator and balance them with the level controls. Set an interval with osc-2 detune (e.g. +7 st = fifth); transpose the pair with tune and confirm the interval stays.","osc","Two blended WT oscillators; transposing keeps the interval"),
  ("Learn the per-osc animator: raise position depth to scan from the current base position on each note (negative depth scans backwards). Choose a one-shot envelope shape or a looping, tempo-synced LFO shape; set speed. The same animator can also drive level.","animator","Position visibly moves on each note trigger"),
  ("Reese bass: identical saw tables, osc-2 detune ~15 cents (or ~10 for subtler beating), lower tune slightly to darken. Set both to similar start positions, then positive position depth on osc 1 and negative on osc 2 so they move in opposite directions, subtle depth, slow speed.","reese","Thick beating bass with gentle opposing timbral motion and a solid low end"),
  ("Harmony lead: saw on osc 1 and a metallic FM-style table on osc 2, detune −5 st (fourth / inverted fifth). Put a ramp-up one-shot shape on osc 2's level at a slow speed so the harmony fades in. Scan the table first (this one gets brighter toward the end), start osc 2 partway in, and add positive position depth so it brightens as it enters. Osc 1 gets a slow, deep triangle loop on position plus a little level modulation from the same shape.","harmony","Chords bloom: harmony fades in and brightens rather than hitting at once"),
  ("Optionally layer the track LFO subtly on position, a subtle pitch-envelope bend, a low-pass filter effect with slow modulation, and a reverb send; sequence per-step changes of modulation speed/depth for rhythmic variation.","finish","More alive, controlled top end"),
  ("Atmospheric pad: airy/hollow table on osc 1, noisy/aggressive table on osc 2. Tempo-synced sample-and-hold on osc-1 position (jumps each step), faster; S&H on osc 2 at a slower speed (use the random/smooth shape instead for gentle drift). Detune osc 2 to +19 st (compound fifth) and lower its level with level modulation. Modulate osc-1 position depth and S&H speed so the steps alternate between slow subtle moves and busy bursts. Add the LP filter effect and reverb. Each chord note gets its own modulation.","pad","Rhythmic yet unpredictable, generative atmosphere"),
  ("Pluck: main sound on osc 2 with a looping ramp-down shape on level, medium speed, full depth and low base level for a quick decay. Set base position near the bright part with positive depth so each note starts from the softer tone (or the reverse for a bright attack); compensate lost energy with overdrive. Layer a noisy table on osc 1 with the same level shape and speed. Use a long amp decay/release for a delay-like trail, then reverb and an LFO on pan.","pluck","Plucky WT voice with trailing tails"),
  ("Texture variant: load a sample (vocal, field recording) in place of a wavetable and pitch it down a few octaves; the same tune/position/modulation tools give a glitchy texture.","sample-as-wt","Recognisable sample character with a glitchy, granular-like quality")],
 ["Transposing with tune leaves the osc-1/osc-2 interval unchanged.","Reese bass beats audibly; returning detune to 0 removes the beating.","In the harmony lead the second voice fades in and brightens over the note.","The S&H pad alternates between sparse and busy motion when its speed is modulated."],
 ["wavetable","dual-oscillator","detune","reese","sample-and-hold","pluck","pad","modulation","web-audio"],
 note="Groovebox OS feature tutorial; hardware button paths dropped, recipes mapped to generic dual-WT modules. Factory wavetable names omitted.")
